# grid_paths:
# CSES 1638, Grid Paths I (https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1638), in our own
# words: a square grid whose squares are free or traps; the number of ways to go
# from the top left square to the bottom right square by steps right and down
# that never enter a trap (zero when either end is a trap), modulo 10^9 + 7. The
# input is the grid as a Scott list of rows, each a Scott list of Scott
# booleans, true for a free square, false for a trap. Part of the CSES port's
# second batch.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition: the list of all paths from the top
# left square of a grid is built (none when that square is a trap, the empty path
# when the grid is that one square, otherwise each path of the grid without its
# first row with a step down put in front, and each path of the grid without its
# first column with a step right put in front, a step being a Scott boolean), and
# its length is counted in binary and reduced modulo 10^9 + 7. Its reading is
# exponential in the grid even under tabling, since the paths themselves are built
# and counted; the replacement the family asks for is the dynamic program
# ways(row, column) = ways(row - 1, column) + ways(row, column - 1) over the squares, on fixed-width
# integers modulo 10^9 + 7.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ and application
# only: its text holds no top, no choice, no parallel and no literal (the modulus
# and the numerals are written with binary_zero, binary_even and binary_odd). It
# β-reduces to V, the canonical binary numeral of the answer: every recursion
# ends, since paths consumes the grid, a finite list of finite lists that loses a
# row or a column at each call, and every other recursion consumes a finite list
# or a binary numeral. So the β reduction reaches V; the branches that a Scott
# boolean discards are never read. ../common/binary_modular's binary_modulo, which
# writes no top, is called with the positive modulus only. So ⊢_β P = V, and
# P ≃_O V by the soundness of the derivable equations for the contextual preorder
# of Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V is an
# answer, P ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V ⊑_O W,
# while Böhm's theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W to
# λx y. y, and applying both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the
# second not.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "fixpoint")
λ fixpoint
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "boolean")
λ true false _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons append map _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "natural")
λ binary_zero binary_even binary_odd _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "arithmetic")
λ merge_binary_increment _ _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "cses") "common") "binary_modular")
λ _ _ _ binary_modulo
. (λtuple. tuple λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd binary_zero)
λ modulus
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple λ. fixpoint λself count items. items count λ_ tail. self (merge_binary_increment count) tail
  )
λ count_from
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ. fixpoint
    λ self rows
    . rows nil
      λ row others
      . row nil
        λ free rest
        . free
          ( ( λ down right
              . others
                (rest (cons nil nil) λ_ _. right)
                λ_ _. append down λ. right
            )
            ( others nil λ_ _. map (cons true) λ. self others )
            ( rest nil λ_ _. map (cons false) λ. self λ. map (λgrid_row. grid_row nil λ_ tail. tail) rows )
          ) nil
  )
λ paths
. (λtuple. tuple λrows. binary_modulo (count_from binary_zero λ. paths rows) modulus)
λ grid_paths
. export grid_paths
